p.src:
Now comes the crisis. Suppose $20\%$ of the corporate-loan portfolio defaults, so the bank writes the loans down from $100 to $80. Then the assets become $\begin{aligned}
A &= 80 \text{ corporate loans} + 20 \text{ cash} = 100.\end{aligned}$ The deposit liability is still $100. Therefore the only thing that can give way is common equity: $\begin{aligned}
L &= 100 \text{ transaction account}, \\
E_{\text{common}} &\to 0.\end{aligned}$

